(verb
  :name "delete!"
  :std "SRFI-1"
  :library "list"
  :tier "core"
  :kind "destructive"
  :signature "(delete! x lst [pred]) -> list"
  :summary "Removes all elements equal to x from lst, reusing its cons cells and returning the modified list. The original binding is destroyed."
  :explanation "The destructive version of delete that modifies its input list in place rather than creating a new one. It removes all elements equal to x (under an optional predicate) by unlinking their cons cells, which means the original list is destroyed - any reference to it will now see the modified structure. The return value is the only reliable thing; the original variable should not be used afterwards. This operation preserves circularity if the list was circular, unlike the pure version which always returns a fresh list. The failure mode is when code continues using the original variable after calling delete!, finding a list that has been cut or overwritten under it. Implementations that quietly return a fresh list hide this bug, which is why it went uncaught. For proper lists, every cell in the result must have been part of the original list."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(define lst (list 1 2 3 2 4))\n(delete! 2 lst)" :note "PENDING-ENGINE")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(define lst (list 'a 'b 'c 'b 'd))\n(define result (delete! 'b lst))\n(list lst result)" :note "PENDING-ENGINE")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(define lst (list 10 20 30 20 40))\n(delete! 20 lst <)\n(display lst)\n(newline)" :note "PENDING-ENGINE")
  )
  :caveats ("Destructive operation - the original list is modified and should not be used afterwards." "The predicate version requires the third argument to be a procedure of two arguments." "Circular lists remain circular after deletion, but improper lists become proper lists if the element to delete was the last one.")
  :drawbacks ("Cannot be used in parallel contexts where the original list must remain intact." "Requires careful handling of variables to avoid accidental reuse of the destroyed list.")
  :usecases ("Removing all occurrences of a specific value from a list in place." "Cleaning up a list by removing unwanted elements without allocation overhead." "Modifying a list structure while preserving its overall shape.")
  :related ("delete" "delete-duplicates!" "remove" "member")
  :learn (:concept "In-place modification of cons cells" :prerequisites ("cons" "car" "cdr") :progression "Learn about destructive operations and how they differ from pure functions. Understand how cons cells are reused in destructive operations.")
  :provenance "SRFI-1 §Linear update; implemented lib/std/srfi-1.js:954; documented against observed runtime behavior 2026-08-03"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define lst (list 1 2 3 2 4))\n(define result (delete! 2 lst))\n(display (list 'original lst 'result result)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (delete-check x lst)\n  (let ((cells (pair-fold cons '() lst))\n        (result (delete! x lst)))\n    (and (equal? result (filter (lambda (y) (not (equal? y x))) lst))\n         (every (lambda (cell) (memq cell cells)) (pair-fold cons '() result)))))\n(display (map delete-check '(1 2) (list (list 1 2 3 2) (list 'a 'b 'c 'b))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define lst (list 'a 'b 'c 'b 'd))\n(define result (delete! 'b lst))\n(display (list 'eq?-original (eq? lst (list 'a 'b 'c 'b 'd)) 'eq?-result (eq? result (delete 'b (list 'a 'b 'c 'b 'd)))))\n(newline)"
     :problem "The operator wants to remove all elements equal to x from a list, modifying the original list structure rather than creating a new one. The operator asks: which verb removes elements in place and reuses the existing cons cells?"
     :explanation "eng: The original list is modified and should not be used afterwards. math: The result must be equal to the filtered list, and every cell in the result must have been part of the original list. cs: The eq? test shows that the original binding is destroyed - it no longer refers to the same list structure."
     :meta "The destructive operation reuses existing cons cells, so the result shares structure with the original list.")
    (:eng "Destructive operations modify their arguments in place, destroying the original binding." :math "The result must be structurally equivalent to the filtered list and share cells with the original." :cs "eq? tests show the original binding is destroyed after modification.")
  )
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Removing elements from a sequence by unlinking their cons cells."
     :rungs (
       (:domain "list" :code "(define lst (list 1 2 3 2 4))\n(delete! 2 lst)")
       (:domain "removing bad apples from a basket" :code "(define basket (list 'apple 'rotten-apple 'banana 'rotten-apple 'orange))\n(delete! 'rotten-apple basket)")
       (:domain "cleaning up a database query result" :code "(define results (list 'active 'inactive 'active 'pending 'active))\n(delete! 'inactive results)")
     )
     :explanation "In each case, elements matching a criterion are removed by unlinking their cons cells. The operation preserves the overall structure while removing specific elements."
     :meta "The shape transfers because element removal is a fundamental sequence operation.")
    (:eng "Destructive operations modify the original structure rather than creating new ones." :math "Element removal is a basic sequence operation that can be implemented destructively." :cs "Cons cell reuse is the key property of destructive operations.")
  )
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(define lst (list 1 2 3 2 4))\n(define result (delete! 2 lst))\n(display (list 'original-cells (pair-fold cons '() lst) 'result-cells (pair-fold cons '() result)))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define (cell-preservation? x lst)\n  (let ((cells (pair-fold cons '() lst))\n        (result (delete! x lst)))\n    (every (lambda (cell) (memq cell cells)) (pair-fold cons '() result))))\n(display (map cell-preservation? '(1 2) (list (list 1 2 3 2) (list 'a 'b 'c 'b))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define lst (list 'a 'b 'c 'b 'd))\n(define result (delete! 'b lst))\n(display (list 'original-eq? (eq? lst (list 'a 'b 'c 'b 'd)) 'result-eq? (eq? result (delete 'b (list 'a 'b 'c 'b 'd)))))\n(newline)"
     :claim "Every cons cell in the result must have been part of the original list. The original binding is destroyed after modification."
     :invariant "forall x, lst. |delete!(x, lst)| = filter(not equal? x) lst and every cell in delete!(x, lst) ∈ pair-fold cons '() lst"
     :meta "The proof shows that the result shares structure with the original list and that the original binding is no longer valid.")
    (:eng "Destructive operations modify their arguments in place." :math "Cell preservation is the key property of destructive operations." :cs "eq? tests show that the original binding is destroyed.")
  )
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (clean-list lst bad-elements)\n  (fold (lambda (x acc)\n           (if (memq x bad-elements) acc (cons x acc)))\n         '() lst))\n(define (clean-list! lst bad-elements)\n  (let ((result (reverse lst)))\n    (while (memq (car result) bad-elements)\n      (set-cdr! result (cdr result)))\n    result))\n(display (list (clean-list (list 1 2 3 2 4) '(2 3)) (clean-list! (list 1 2 3 2 4) '(2 3)))))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (remove-all x lst)\n  (if (null? lst) '()\n      (if (equal? x (car lst))\n          (remove-all x (cdr lst))\n          (cons (car lst) (remove-all x (cdr lst))))))\n(define (remove-all! x lst)\n  (let loop ((current lst))\n    (if (null? current) '()\n        (if (equal? x (car current))\n            (loop (cdr current))\n            (let ((rest (remove-all! x (cdr current))))\n              (set-cdr! current rest)\n              current)))))\n(display (list (remove-all 2 (list 1 2 3 2 4)) (remove-all! 2 (list 1 2 3 2 4)))))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (delete-multiple! lst xs)\n  (let ((result lst))\n    (for-each (lambda (x)\n               (while (and (not (null? result)) (equal? x (car result)))\n                 (set! result (cdr result))))\n            xs)\n    result))\n(display (delete-multiple! (list 1 2 3 2 4) '(2 3)))\n(newline)"
     :base "delete! removes elements equal to x from a list by unlinking their cons cells."
     :emergent "eng: clean-list and clean-list! show the pure vs destructive approaches. math: remove-all and remove-all! demonstrate recursive vs iterative destructive removal. cs: delete-multiple! shows how to extend the operation to remove multiple values."
     :meta "The composition shows different ways to implement element removal, both destructively and non-destructively.")
    (:eng "Destructive operations can be more efficient when modification in place is acceptable." :math "Recursive and iterative approaches can both implement destructive operations." :cs "Multiple elements can be removed by chaining destructive operations.")
  )
)
